Expose the running machine to an embedder, so a caller can read what a run
cost instead of parsing what it printed.

Against MP-SPDZ 0.4.3 (6 Jul 2026). Applies to one file.

Why this is needed at all
-------------------------
Everything the shim wants is already in `BaseMachine::comm_stats`: rounds and
bytes, broken out by named channel. Two things stand between a caller and it.

  * `BaseMachine::singleton` is not cleared by `~BaseMachine()`, so reading it
    after the run returns reads freed memory. Seven parties segfault at once.
  * The `Machine<T, U>` that holds the statistics is constructed as a temporary
    in `run_with_error` and destroyed on the same line, so there is no moment
    at which an outside caller holds a live one.

So the statistics have to be taken from inside the run, and the only place that
is inside is here.

What it does
------------
Declares an initially-null callback and calls it, if an embedder registered it,
with the machine still alive. A stock build registers nothing, the branch is
not taken, and the binary behaves exactly as before --- which is the property
that matters: the numbers this yields have to be numbers about stock MP-SPDZ,
not about a fork of it. The callback avoids the platform-specific linker rules
for undefined weak symbols in normal party binaries.

The temporary is given a name. That is the only change to the run itself, and
it extends the machine's life to the end of the block rather than the end of
the statement.

Upstream
--------
Worth offering: the hook is three lines, costs a null check per run, and is the
smallest thing that turns MP-SPDZ into something an embedder can measure. A
general version would name the hook for MP-SPDZ rather than for this project,
and would probably prefer a registered callback over a weak symbol.

diff --git a/Processor/OnlineMachine.hpp b/Processor/OnlineMachine.hpp
